WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.078
A feed is a type of Task.

00:00:02.108 --> 00:00:06.257
A feed maintenance Task finds new and active projects, labels them,

00:00:06.287 --> 00:00:10.949
and stores them as Task Objects, so the next agent can reuse them.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:12.405
Why does this help?

00:00:12.538 --> 00:00:15.737
A model alone doesn't know what came out after its training.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:21.507
A feed gives an agent a short list to read, with a source, and a license where it's known.

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:23.389
This is a real feed page.

00:00:23.419 --> 00:00:27.232
There are five, on agent harnesses, evaluation frameworks,

00:00:27.262 --> 00:00:31.436
memory systems, document reading, and private data redaction.

00:00:31.883 --> 00:00:37.496
For this page's search, GitHub showed about seventeen hundred new repositories in two days.

00:00:37.688 --> 00:00:41.862
These pages show their November first run, on our own machine.

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:45.296
Here's a separate, newer feed, of skill packs and tools.

00:00:45.371 --> 00:00:48.489
It runs every four hours, as an OmniNode.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:50.485
It searches public GitHub.

00:00:50.515 --> 00:00:52.193
Code checks the license.

00:00:52.326 --> 00:00:54.561
Reuse skips what hasn't changed.

00:00:54.822 --> 00:00:58.079
Muse Spark or Muse Wonder labels the rest.

00:00:58.433 --> 00:01:00.260
A rule tests each label.

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:04.560
Then a separate worker audits it, and only approved items are published.

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:06.325
This is one real item.

00:01:06.355 --> 00:01:09.703
It has its source, its license, and a short summary.

00:01:09.733 --> 00:01:13.420
It names the model that labeled it, and the model that audited it.

00:01:13.681 --> 00:01:16.357
Any agent can read it, with no key.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:19.596
After six runs, it lists fifteen projects.

00:01:19.787 --> 00:01:23.218
Those six runs, with their audits, cost about twenty cents.

00:01:23.409 --> 00:01:26.190
It's a small feed, and we're starting small.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:27.610
Do feeds help?

00:01:27.871 --> 00:01:31.952
We asked thirty questions about recent repositories on the five pages.

00:01:32.143 --> 00:01:35.296
A plain agent, told not to guess, got none.

00:01:35.556 --> 00:01:38.059
With Teleion Fabric, twenty-six.

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:42.679
The four misses were rows on pages it didn't open within its six tool calls.

00:01:42.871 --> 00:01:46.184
On repositories not on any feed, it got none of six.

00:01:46.214 --> 00:01:51.190
And a plain agent with GitHub's API got all thirty, on fewer tokens.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:56.598
So a feed only knows what it lists, and on these lookups, a direct search beat it.

00:01:56.952 --> 00:02:00.418
Reading the feeds also cost more tokens in this test.

00:02:00.865 --> 00:02:05.817
Our hope is that one shared list costs less than every agent searching alone.

00:02:06.008 --> 00:02:07.976
We haven't measured that yet.

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:09.279
Feeds are Tasks.

00:02:09.309 --> 00:02:12.426
Their results are Task Objects, ready to reuse.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:15.747
Connect your agents at teleionlabs.com.

